From 6cb4b46234fa8ca44906a4fa2486ccf42c12b96a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 12:14:10 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08053/11362] The empty intersection was the depth cap, not a
 dynamics-vs-plannability conflict

Best-of-N found criterion (iii) = 0 at every N and diagnosed it as an EMPTY INTERSECTION: 9 of
40 stall candidates clear held-out dynamics, 2 reach a satisfiable goal with a found plan, and
no candidate is in both sets. The reading on offer was that selecting on dynamics is
anti-selective for plannability -- a real design tension to be resolved against one criterion or
the other. It is not. The gate's own census said 6 of those 9 were rejected UNDECIDED as
`goal_unreached_within_depth`, and that is the whole effect.

Re-running the SHIPPED goal gate and planner over the same 48 frozen completions, sweeping
max_depth and nothing else:

  depth   (i)  plan  BOTH
     40     9     2     0     <- shipped
     61     9     6     3
     80     9     6     3
    120     9     6     3
    200     9     6     3

The intersection fills at 61 and saturates immediately. Three of the four conversions -- tn36
k0, k2, k6 -- are strict-dynamics TRUE *and already pass the shipped trust gate*: engines the
agent had accepted and then vetoed for no reason but the cap. All four plans are exactly 61
actions in ~2226 nodes, against a 20000-node budget.

THE COST IS NOT SMALL, IT IS ABSENT. Mean planner wall time 0.357s at depth 40, 0.339 at 61,
0.334 at 80, 0.355 at 200 -- flat within noise, and lowest where the conversions happen, because
a search that succeeds stops early instead of draining its frontier. Phase 2's independent
greedy rollout agrees by a different method: the four reach goal_depth 61 for 366-578 engine
calls against 17854 production-affordable.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT SAY. The other 34 stall candidates are not depth-capped and raising the cap
does nothing for them -- Phase 2's taxonomy has them as 13 inert_at_root, 8 disproved at an
engine fixed point, 4 cycling, 9 unrunnable/timeout. That is broken generated code, a different
problem. And every conversion here is tn36; no other game has a candidate whose goal is
reachable at ANY swept depth, so this is one game's mechanic (fill 61 cells of a row, one click
each), not a corpus-wide result. Reported as "the cap manufactured a conflict that was not
there", not as "deeper search solves induction".

Gate and planner are swept TOGETHER, always. arc_llm_reinduction.py states the invariant: the
gate's depth veto is sound only because the planner it guards shares the cap. Deepening the gate
alone converts an honest veto into a false accept one layer down. max_nodes is untouched on
both, so a candidate terminating budget_exhausted is recorded UNDECIDED and excluded from the
conversion count -- a node-budget effect must not be reported as a depth effect. `goal_energy`
is not supplied, matching best-of-N, so every count is a LOWER bound on the live best-first
planner.

CONTROL: depth 40 reproduces best-of-N on 47 of 48. The single difference is ft09|5, the
non-terminating engine that wedged the generation loop for 13 minutes -- best-of-N recorded
`gate_timeout` because it scores criterion (i) first and that engine does not terminate under
WorldModelVerifier; this sweep skips criterion (i) as depth-invariant and joins it in, so it
reaches the gate, which returns immediately on a completion that defines no is_level_complete.
Any other mismatch would have invalidated the run.

No GPU, no generation: the completions are frozen upstream and re-read. Every execution of
generated code is in a killable subprocess -- an in-process alarm would be swallowed by the
broad `except Exception` in the search loops and become a silent false CLEAN. The verdict and
the saturation flag are DERIVED from the table rather than hand-written, so they cannot outlive
the numbers that justify them. The post-lint reformat was proven inert by re-running the scorer:
reproducibility_checksum df9e0317... unchanged.

adversarial_verify: 0 flagged.

